WEBVTT

00:00.800 --> 00:03.780
Currently, we waste about one-third of

00:03.780 --> 00:05.840
everything that's produced, and that's
globally.

00:08.700 --> 00:12.000
Now, I believe this is going to become one
of the biggest issues.

00:12.000 --> 00:13.060
Right now, when they

00:13.060 --> 00:17.480
go to the climate summits, they aren't
talking about food waste, but this is

00:17.480 --> 00:20.320
where we could really make a big
difference. We waste one-third of

00:20.320 --> 00:26.160
everything we produce. In rich countries,
it's because food is so cheap, we can

00:26.160 --> 00:31.180
throw it away, right? Servings are
great big. Go to one of these restaurants,

00:31.180 --> 00:34.820
the platter comes out, it's an amazing
amount of food. You might not eat all of

00:34.820 --> 00:38.120
it. It gets discarded. In poorer
countries, it's because there's no

00:38.120 --> 00:41.380
infrastructure to get the crops out of the
field. It's because there's no

00:41.380 --> 00:49.100
refrigeration, so the stuff spoils. So we
produce one-third more than we really need

00:49.100 --> 00:53.620
right now with food waste. In other words,
we grow enough food to feed everybody.

00:53.680 --> 00:57.340
It's a matter of economics,
infrastructure, and distribution. This is

00:57.340 --> 01:00.360
something that's going to be, I think, a
bigger issue moving forward, and it

01:00.360 --> 01:05.800
wouldn't hurt agriculture to hop on this
issue. When we're being attacked about the

01:05.800 --> 01:09.420
amount of water we use in California's
dairies, or the amount of methane that we

01:09.420 --> 01:13.640
produce, or the amount of diesel that we
burn, maybe we should go right to food

01:13.640 --> 01:17.940
waste and say, yes, but consumer, this is
on you. All we're doing is producing the

01:17.940 --> 01:19.760
food. It's not us that's wasting it.

01:20.000 --> 01:28.900
[music]
